one million deaths a grim milestone for  a pandemic that's not even a year old  in 10 months more people have died from  covet 19 than from  hiv malaria the flu and cholera  combined a hefty body count for a  microscopic pathogen  and some experts think it's an  undercount  the pandemic isn't done yet cases are  spiking in india and europe  complacency and frustration are growing  people had a fear of the coronavirus in  march and april  but they don't anymore some are also  leaving their homes because they have to  go to work  they don't have any money and they don't  have any other options  as the numbers continue to rise how long  before the next  milestone  this is dw's coveted 19 special hello  and welcome to the show i'm steven  beardsley in berlin  1 million deaths in 10 months what began  as a mysterious respiratory disease in  wuhan china  has now spread around the world leaving  a significant body count in its wake  and showing little sign of abating in  the near future here's a look  at how we reach this point  the first death outside of china was  reported in the philippines  on february 2nd just 12 days later  france confirmed the first victim in  europe  at the end of february the united states  reported its first death  on june 28 the global death toll hit  half a million  and just three months later the  millionth victim was confirmed  the u.s has the highest death toll so  far  it also has the highest number of cases  followed by  india and brazil spain has the most  confirmed infections in the european  union  with cases surging across the world  experts expect the global death toll  to climb rapidly in the coming months  [Music]  now one of the country's worst hit by  the pandemic wasn't on that last list  peru which has topped 800 000 infections  has had one of the world's highest  mortality rates and its economy has  shrunk by a whopping 30 percent  but new cases are slowing and that's  prompted the government to begin easing  restrictions  many fear that they're acting too soon  finally able to be by her husband's side  this desert cemetery in northern peru is  the final resting place for local  coronavirus victims  now that restrictions have been eased  relatives are allowed in to visit  even though it isn't at the spot anna  maria and her husband had planned for  me when i went to town to make burial  arrangements  they told me i couldn't bury him in the  plot we had chosen  i was told that an order had been issued  that everyone who died from kovid  had to be buried in this cemetery  others are still fighting for their  lives peru's president says the  country's health care system is stronger  now  than it was at the beginning of the  pandemic  and the government is expanding testing  including in low-income areas of the  capital lima  so with the rate of new infections on  the decline  the government is pushing ahead with  plans to reopen more of the economy  and even start letting in international  flights starting with neighboring  countries  but some health experts are warning  against reopening too fast  reopening gyms bars discos and  other forms of entertainment will be  terrible  so yes it could bring about a second  wave quicker  i think we definitely need to reconsider  these steps  the fear is that peru could repeat the  pattern of other countries  seeing a rise in cases followed by a new  wave  of mass burials  i'm joined now by alan lopez he's an  epidemiologist and professor at the  university of melbourne  which is where he joins me from  professor it's good to have you on the  show  you've said that this death count could  actually be a vast undercount  by how much  well it's hard to say but it's probably  likely to be somewhere between 30 and 60  percent of an undercount due to a number  of factors  why is that what are those factors  well there's four principal factors  stephen that i think are driving the  numbers down one is that doctors are  still learning how to apply the who  rules to certify  uh covert deaths and this they're  getting used to that until they get used  to it properly they'll be under counting  deaths  secondly covert 19  kills people in ways that we're still  learning about in other words that they  magnifies up the background risks if  you've got hypertension  you're a smoker if you're overweight if  you've got chronic lung disease  then it's likely that covert 19 is going  to be more serious for you  and will kill many of the people with  those conditions those co-morbidities  thirdly uh covert 19 is having a big  impact on health services  so in developing poor countries we're  seeing  programs essential programs like hiv  control tv  malaria are all being affected and so  many of the people who need those  services are not getting them  and are probably dying as a result and  even in rich countries  we see people not turning up for cancer  screening when they should be  and finally and perhaps most importantly  many many deaths in the world go  uncounted about  one-third of deaths go unregistered each  year  and even many of those that are  registered don't get a proper  correct cause of death so it's likely  that covert 19 suffers from all those  conditions as well  you mentioned there the comorbidities  can we say if there's a profile to the  average coronavirus death from what we  know about the death count so far  although the main thing that drives  covet that defines covert 19 deaths his  age  the vast majority of people who die from  covert 19  uh die above the age of 70 and and the  sex rape show from countries where we  can count it reliably  suggests that about uh for every female  uh who dies there's about three males  so we got a fairly clear idea of the age  and sex  composition of mortality are we slowing  down the death rate since we've  been going at this for about 10 months  now have we learned enough to slow it  down  the answer is no we have not slowed it  down i don't know whether we've learned  enough to do that but we certainly  haven't applied it  death rates are rising we're seeing  second waves in several countries in  europe  we've seen those in my country in  australia deaths are rising  uh they're rising steadily and they're  going to rise a lot further  when should we expect the next milestone  say 2 million 3 million do you have a  prediction  it's always difficult to predict but my  prediction is that  by the end of this year by december 31st  we'll probably see at least two million  possibly as high as two and a half  million  cobra 19 deaths worldwide professor are  deaths the best way to understand  the impact of this disease in the  pandemic  i don't think so i think the actual  numbers of deaths as frightening as they  are and as frightening as they're going  to be  don't tell the full story colbot 19  kills people primarily at older ages and  in terms of preventive  priorities that public health need to be  concerned about we talk about the number  of years of life  lost on average from from each death and  in terms of covert 19 most of those who  die died older ages and so they tend to  lose  much less years of life than for example  a comparable number of deaths  road traffic accidents for example kills  1.2 million people  every year vast majority of those are a  lot younger than the covert deaths and  so you could argue  that road traffic accidents ought to be  getting at least as much public health  attention  for prevention as covert cobra19 all  right alan lopez professor  and epidemiologist from the university  of melbourne thank you very much  thank you  well our science correspondent derek  williams has been busy looking into your  questions  about the pandemic over now to derek  many of the social controls put in place  are based on asymptomatic transmission  what evidence is there for such a  phenomenon  do people who are asymptomatic transmit  sauroscope 2 to others and if so  then how infectious are they um this has  been one of those issues that's proven  very difficult to come to grips with in  the course of this pandemic for for a  couple of reasons  first of all because we only really  recently began to detect  asymptomatic people on a wide scale um  second because today's asymptomatic  patient  can turn out to be only pre-symptomatic  and and start showing symptoms tomorrow  and we're pretty sure that  pre-symptomatic carriers  can be highly infectious um third  because the viral load an asymptomatic  patient carries  is is usually very similar to that of  someone  showing symptoms so so what evidence is  there that people who test  positive for covid19 but don't ever  develop  symptoms how likely is it that they can  infect others  a pre-print paper set for publication  soon shows  how tricky it can be to prove but but  the researchers think they did it on a  flight from  italy to south korea carrying nearly 300  passengers who at the time  displayed no covet 19 symptoms before  boarding  all of them were given n95 masks and  most  wore them throughout the flight except  at meal times and when they went to the  bathroom  when the passengers arrived and sold  they were quarantined  straight away for two weeks and all of  them were tested on the first day  and the last day of that quarantine six  people  tested positive for covet 19 that first  day of quarantine  but they never developed symptoms they  were confirmed asymptomatic carriers  but one woman who tested negative on the  first day of quarantine  developed symptoms on day 8 of it she  had sat  a few rows away from an asymptomatic  patient and used  the same bathroom as them because of the  timing and what we know about the  viruses  incubation period the researchers  concluded that she was very likely  infected  by one of the asymptomatic passengers on  board the plane  sounds kind of like a sherlock holmes  mystery doesn't it  um it just goes to show you though how  difficult it is  to acquire and interpret this kind of  evidence  many studies have tried but so far none  of them  has been clear-cut enough really for big  health authorities like the who  or the cdc to take a stand on the issue
 

